  <body>

    <Header />

    <main class="journal-story-page">

      <!-- BREADCRUMB -->

      <div class="product-breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span>·</span>
        <a href="/#journal">Journal</a>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>How to Create a Mediterranean Table</span>
      </div>


      <!-- HERO -->

      <section class="journal-story-hero">

        <p class="product-detail-eyebrow">
          TABLE & ENTERTAINING
        </p>

        <h1>
          How to Create a<br />
          Mediterranean Table
        </h1>

        <p class="journal-story-intro">
          A relaxed Mediterranean table is built from warmth, texture,
          natural materials and a few carefully chosen details.
        </p>

      </section>


      <!-- HERO IMAGE -->

      <section class="journal-story-cover">

        <img
          src="/images/categories/category-tableware.png"
          alt="Mediterranean table setting with natural textures and warm ceramics"
        />

      </section>


      <!-- 01 -->

      <section class="journal-story-row journal-story-divider">

        <div class="journal-story-copy">

          <p class="journal-story-number">
            01
          </p>

          <h2>
            Start with natural materials
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Mediterranean look begins with materials that feel relaxed and
            tactile. Linen, woven fibres, wood, stone and ceramics create warmth
            before anything decorative is added. Their natural textures catch the
            light differently throughout the day and give even a very simple table
            a sense of depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Instead of a perfectly pressed formal tablecloth, try a textured linen
            runner, woven placemats or even a bare wooden surface. Mix smooth
            ceramics with slightly imperfect handmade finishes and let the materials
            speak for themselves. The goal is softness and character, not perfection.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="journal-story-mood">

          <img
            src="/images/categories/category-kitchen.png"
            alt="Natural kitchen textures with ceramics, wood and woven materials"
          />

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- 02 -->

      <section class="journal-story-row journal-story-row-product journal-story-divider">

        <div class="journal-inline-product">

          <a href="/new-finds/fanquare-blue-floral-dessert-plates">
            <img
              src="/images/new-finds/fanquare-blue-floral-dessert-plates.png"
              alt="Blue floral dessert plates by fanquare"
            />
          </a>

          <div class="journal-inline-product-info">

            <p class="journal-story-product-category">
              TABLEWARE
            </p>

            <h3>
              Blue Floral Dessert Plates
            </h3>

            <p class="journal-story-product-brand">
              fanquare
            </p>

            <a
              href="/new-finds/fanquare-blue-floral-dessert-plates"
              class="journal-story-discover"
            >
              Discover →
            </a>

          </div>

        </div>


        <div class="journal-story-copy">

          <p class="journal-story-number">
            02
          </p>

          <h2>
            Layer ceramics instead of matching everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            A Mediterranean table feels more personal when every piece does not
            come from the same set. Combining different shapes, patterns and subtle
            variations in colour creates the feeling of a table collected gradually
            over time rather than arranged straight from a catalogue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pair softly coloured dinner plates with patterned dessert plates,
            handmade-looking bowls or serving pieces with visible texture. Look for
            one or two colours that appear quietly across several pieces — perhaps
            blue, sage green or warm ivory. That small repetition is enough to make
            an eclectic table feel intentional and beautifully connected.
          </p>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- 03 -->

      <section class="journal-story-row journal-story-row-product-reverse journal-story-divider">

        <div class="journal-story-copy">

          <p class="journal-story-number">
            03
          </p>

          <h2>
            Add coloured glass
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coloured glassware can transform a simple table with almost no effort.
            Green, amber and smoky shades catch natural light beautifully and add
            another layer of colour without making the setting feel overly styled.
            They work especially well beside warm ceramics, natural linen and the
            soft neutral tones typical of Mediterranean interiors.
          </p>

          <p>
            You do not need a full matching set. One distinctive glass at each place
            setting can be enough to change the mood of the entire table. Choose a
            textured or softly coloured design that feels special in the hand — the
            kind of small detail guests notice when they sit down.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="journal-inline-product">

          <a href="/kasodi-edit/boston-green-wine-glass">
            <img
              src="/images/kasodi-edit/kasodi-edit-villeroy-boch-boston-green-glass.png"
              alt="Boston green wine glass by Villeroy and Boch"
            />
          </a>

          <div class="journal-inline-product-info">

            <p class="journal-story-product-category">
              GLASSWARE
            </p>

            <h3>
              Boston Green Wine Glass
            </h3>

            <p class="journal-story-product-brand">
              Villeroy & Boch
            </p>

            <a
              href="/kasodi-edit/boston-green-wine-glass"
              class="journal-story-discover"
            >
              Discover →
            </a>

          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- 04 -->

      <section class="journal-story-row journal-story-row-product journal-story-divider">

        <div class="journal-inline-product">

          <a href="/kasodi-edit/botanical-vintage-pitcher">
            <img
              src="/images/kasodi-edit/kasodi-edit-floral-ceramic-pitcher.png"
              alt="Botanical vintage pitcher by Ethereal Eves"
            />
          </a>

          <div class="journal-inline-product-info">

            <p class="journal-story-product-category">
              HOME DECOR
            </p>

            <h3>
              Botanical Vintage Pitcher
            </h3>

            <p class="journal-story-product-brand">
              Ethereal Eves
            </p>

            <a
              href="/kasodi-edit/botanical-vintage-pitcher"
              class="journal-story-discover"
            >
              Discover →
            </a>

          </div>

        </div>


        <div class="journal-story-copy">

          <p class="journal-story-number">
            04
          </p>

          <h2>
            Bring in something botanical
          </h2>

          <p>
            A centrepiece does not need to be elaborate. Olive branches, fresh herbs,
            garden flowers or a few leafy stems bring movement and life to the table
            without making it feel formal. Choose something loose and slightly
            irregular rather than a perfectly arranged bouquet — it feels much more
            at home in a relaxed Mediterranean setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            The vessel matters too. A ceramic pitcher, aged jug or simple handmade
            vase can become part of the composition rather than merely holding the
            flowers. Keep the arrangement low enough for conversation to flow easily
            across the table and allow a few stems to fall naturally instead of
            arranging everything too precisely.
          </p>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- 05 + NOTE -->

      <section class="journal-story-closing">

        <div class="journal-story-ending">

          <p class="journal-story-number">
            05
          </p>

          <h2>
            Let the table feel lived in
          </h2>

          <p>
           The final step is not another object. It is knowing when to stop. Leave
           linen slightly relaxed, allow serving pieces to become part of the
           composition and resist the urge to make every detail perfectly
           symmetrical. A little imperfection is often what makes a table feel
           inviting rather than staged.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about how the table will actually be used: bread being passed,
            glasses moved, serving bowls shared and flowers shifting as people settle
            in. A beautiful table should feel ready for people, food and conversation
            — not just a photograph. That sense of ease is ultimately what gives a
            Mediterranean table its charm.
          </p>

        </div>


        <aside class="journal-story-note">

          <p class="product-detail-eyebrow">
            KASODI NOTE
          </p>

          <h3>
            A table feels more inviting when not every element matches perfectly.
          </h3>

        </aside>

      </section>


